ve to pay interest on that  that has to gradually be repaid  we've got to get the balance right  without at the same time doing what we  can to give people support where there  is a global pressure and we know there  was a global pressure on energy prices  even before putin's invasion of ukraine  that has obviously had a further impact  on costs of living across the globe  energy not least of all so that's why we  put this package in as i say it's worth  about 22 billion pounds we've seen just  this week and i think it was just  yesterday actually the increase in the  national living wage has a beneficial  impact for people the change in the  threshold has a beneficial impact for  people both worth about a thousand  pounds so we've now seen actually the  biggest reduction in taxes a package  that we've seen for about a quarter of a  century and we're putting support into  local authorities reducing fuel duty all  of these things  to try and give people the support we  can up against that pressure that we're  seeing globally and across markets  um it is support that you've given and  you're throwing these big numbers around  and yes they are very big but at the end  of the day this is about people and  people's lives will be put at risk  well that's why we're putting the  support in i absolutely  appreciate we all do the challenge on  this uh all of us are affected by energy  prices i know even this week  where i live where we're on oil fire  heating we i've seen that change  directly in in the price of oil and  actually the ability to get it my home  my family went a few days where we had  no oil just waiting for the for the  suppliers and seen the the the very big  increase in price on that but as i say  it goes we will do everything we can to  give people support with the balance of  what is going on in the wider economy  and making sure we're continuing to  invest and spend in the public sector in  the areas that people want to see it  that's why we've put that big package of  support in chancellor and the prime  minister have both been very clear we'll  continue to look at this  as we go through the year we can't  completely nullify the impacts of the  global markets and global pressure for  example on energy which is obviously the  main focus at the moment  for most people  but we will put in the support that we  can as and when we can as i say looking  at across the board at what we're doing  with the public's money  if if if you're saying that you can  you're doing everything that you can  why don't you tax  the oil and gas companies who are making  so much money  people have to decide between eating and  heating i know we use that phrase so  much but if you say that you are doing  everything you can  why don't you tax them  well i do understand why people look to  that i mean the only thing i would say  labor have been making this point for a  while what they're not able to answer  and  i i think is a is an odd situation is  the reality of what is happening in the  energy market this is first of all this  is a global pressure we're seeing this  around the world so what we do with  companies here in the uk won't have the  impact of changing the whole global  energy market prices but we should also  remember that those energy companies  here in the uk are already paying about  40 percent  tax that's roughly double what most  sectors in the uk economy are paying  they employ hundreds of thousands of  people in the uk we're looking at them  and they are investing hugely um in more  work to develop more energy and produce  more energy they need the income that  they have to make those investments and  the part they play in the economy paying  salaries so a windfall tax on those  companies won't necessarily have the  impact on global prices that people  think it will it can have an impact on  some domestic prices by taxing higher a  sector that is already very very highly  taxed so although that is a very it  sounds an attractive option in the wider  economic context it doesn't really work  and that's why we've looked at what can  we do that will have a beneficial impact  directly for people in their pockets and  that's why this money both the rebates  on council tax the increase in the  national living wage and its threshold  are things that will mean that people  have more money in their pockets to be  able to manage the pressures that  they're finding well if we're talking  about people having more money in their  pockets why don't you restore the 20  pound a week uplift and universal credit  that would give people more money in  their pockets and 20 pounds a week is a  lot of money  yes it is but as i say what we are  looking at is the decisions we have to  make around where we put that support so  we've put that support in by reducing  fuel duty which is a benefit for  everybody at 5p reduction something that  everybody who's using fuel to get around  get to work and back everybody will  benefit from that over the next year  we're putting hundreds of millions of  pounds into local authorities to help  the most vulnerable people with the  support that they need  local authorities know best what they  need in their area in terms of how to  support people we've put that increase  in in the national living wage we put  the increasing in the national insurance  threshold  so everybody who is earning and people  on the national living wage will have an  increase in their pocket  effectively partly through those tax  reductions but also through the increase  in the national living wage so very  targeted at the people who are most in  need  targeted the people who are most in need  but tsukiosama has called it a pathetic  response  well look t kiyosama is is going to play  politics for this he's already as i say  come up with something he thinks sounds  catchy with a windfall tax but in  reality as i've explained doesn't  necessarily and doesn't really work  doesn't answer the problems uh